  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Widespread showers and a few thunderstorms through \nSaturday before drier and less humid airmass briefly settles over \nour region Sunday.\n\nIsolated showers and thunderstorms should diminish this evening\nand early overnight with waning instability via nocturnal\ncooling. However, broad low-level flow around a Bermuda surface\nhigh will carry additional Atlantic and Gulf moisture from the \nSoutheast to the Lower Great Lakes by early Saturday morning, \nwith another round of showers, associated with moist isentropic\nascent ahead of a shortwave trough axis, reaching the western \nSouthern Tier just before daybreak. Outside precipitation,\npartial clearing, weak or calm surface winds, and high humidity\nat/near the surface will likely result in at least patchy steam\nfog over waterways and radiation fog tonight, especially during\nthe predawn hours of Saturday morning through shortly after \ndaybreak. PWAT's are not out of the norm for early August, with \nvalues around 1.5 inches. This moisture combined with MUCAPE \nvalues around 1000 to 2000 J/kg will result in a fair amount of \nshowers and thunderstorms, with periods of heavy rainfall, on \nSaturday. There will likely be more thunderstorms tomorrow than\ntoday, owing to the greater amounts of CAPE. \n\nA weak surface trough will pass across our region Saturday \nafternoon, that combined with an establishing lake breeze \ncirculation, will add additional lift for storms that are already \nblossoming in the peak heating of the afternoon. Severe threat is \nlow, with a deepening warm cloud depth to over 12K feet and 0-6 km \nbulk shear vectors generally in the 20 knot range. But rather the \nrisk for heavy rain and localized drenching downpours would be the \nbigger risk. \n\nStorms linger into the evening hours before a weak trough, and drier \nairmass follows later in the night. This drier airmass will bring \nless humidity Sunday to our region, though afternoon high \ntemperatures will remain similar.  \n\nKey Message 2... Unsettled weather remains for the Lower Great Lakes \nto start the new work week.\n\nA zonal flow early next week will begin to shift southward, with a \nmore noticeable dip late in the week as a deepening upper level low \ndrops southward from Canada. \n\nA moisture rich airmass will remain through the beginning of next \nweek, one that will support not only afternoon convection, but the \npossibility of overnight convection (Monday night into Tuesday), \nthough exact timing of subtle shortwave troughs is with still great \nuncertainty at this point in time. As the cooler and drier upper \nlevel low settles over the eastern Great Lakes region chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms will begin to diminish once the drier \nairmass becomes established.",
